QUERY is a genuinely new HTTP method, standardized in June 2026 as RFC 10008. RFC Editor
The easiest way to understand it is:
QUERY = “GET, but I need to send a request body containing a complex query.”

Why was QUERY needed?
Normally, if you want to retrieve data:
GET /users?name=John&age=40&city=Pune
The query information goes into the URL.
But imagine a very complex search:
{
  "filters": {
    "age": {"min": 30, "max": 50},
    "cities": ["Pune", "Mumbai", "Nagpur"],
    "skills": ["Java", "Selenium", "AWS"]
  },
  "sort": ["experience", "salary"],
  "pagination": {
    "page": 3,
    "size": 100
  }
}
Putting all of that into a GET URL can become huge and awkward. HTTP implementations can also impose URI-size limits. RFC Editor
So historically developers often did:
POST /users/search
Content-Type: application/json

{
   ...complex query...
}
But POST doesn't inherently tell intermediaries that this is a safe read-only query. POST can potentially modify server state and doesn't have the same safe/idempotent semantics as GET. RFC Editor
QUERY fills the gap
Now you can have:
QUERY /users/search
Content-Type: application/json

{
   "age": {"min": 30, "max": 50},
   "cities": ["Pune", "Mumbai"],
   "skills": ["Java", "Selenium"]
}
The important part is:
Method	Request body	Safe?	Idempotent?
GET	❌	✅	✅
POST	✅	potentially ❌	potentially ❌
QUERY	✅	✅	✅


The RFC explicitly defines QUERY as safe and idempotent, while allowing the query itself to be carried in the request content. RFC Editor
Why does "safe + idempotent" matter?
Suppose:
QUERY /products/search
with a complicated body.
Because it's a safe, idempotent operation, infrastructure can potentially:
- retry the request automatically
- cache results
- restart the request
- treat it as a read operation
without worrying that repeating it will create/update/delete something. RFC Editor
One important distinction
QUERY is not simply another version of GET.
GET means roughly:
"Give me the representation of this resource."

QUERY means:
"Perform this query against this resource and give me the result."

The request body defines the query. RFC Editor
And this is particularly useful for things like large/complex searches, filtering, analytics-style queries, and APIs where a GET URL would become unwieldy.
For your API-testing preparation, I would remember this interview sentence:
“HTTP QUERY is a safe and idempotent method designed for read-only queries that need to carry complex query input in the request body, avoiding the limitations of putting everything into a GET URL or semantically using POST for a query.”

Also, an API doesn't automatically support QUERY just because it is now standardized. A server has to implement it; OPTIONS can advertise support through the Allow header. RFC Editor
